WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.169
A useful authorization model names the
relationship being decided.

2
00:00:04.249 --> 00:00:08.234
The inputs usually include an
authenticated subject, a requested action,

3
00:00:08.314 --> 00:00:09.390
and a target object.

4
00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.823
The decision may also depend on ownership,
tenant membership,

5
00:00:12.903 --> 00:00:17.394
organization membership, role, explicit
grants, delegated access, object state,

6
00:00:17.474 --> 00:00:20.637
data classification, or a relationship to
another object.

7
00:00:20.717 --> 00:00:24.892
Different applications legitimately
combine these facts in different ways.

8
00:00:24.972 --> 00:00:29.084
The engineering goal is to make the
relevant facts explicit and trustworthy.

9
00:00:29.164 --> 00:00:33.339
A check such as req dot user dot role
equals user confirms only a broad category.

10
00:00:33.419 --> 00:00:36.329
It does not say whether that user may read
this invoice.

11
00:00:36.409 --> 00:00:40.901
A role can permit an action in principle
while object relationships constrain where

12
00:00:40.981 --> 00:00:42.056
the action applies.

13
00:00:42.136 --> 00:00:44.097
An owner may read a private document.

14
00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:47.593
Any member of the owning organization may
read a shared project.

15
00:00:47.673 --> 00:00:51.026
A project administrator may modify
projects they administer.

16
00:00:51.106 --> 00:00:55.471
A finance approver may approve invoices
only inside the same tenant and only while

17
00:00:55.551 --> 00:00:57.322
an invoice is awaiting approval.

18
00:00:57.402 --> 00:01:01.894
Support personnel may view a restricted
projection without receiving ownership or

19
00:01:01.974 --> 00:01:02.670
edit rights.

20
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:07.051
Write policies in terms that match the
domain rather than compressing them into a

21
00:01:07.131 --> 00:01:08.523
vague is authorized flag.

22
00:01:08.603 --> 00:01:12.525
A policy input might contain the subject
identifier, tenant identifier,

23
00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:16.843
trusted roles, action name, object owner,
object tenant, project membership,

24
00:01:16.923 --> 00:01:17.936
and current state.

25
00:01:18.016 --> 00:01:22.444
The result should default to deny when
required context is absent or inconsistent.

26
00:01:22.524 --> 00:01:27.015
Explicit inputs make it possible to review
whether a policy used client-controlled

27
00:01:27.095 --> 00:01:31.650
values, omitted tenant scope, or confused
a global role with an object relationship.

28
00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:34.450
The policy also needs a defined
enforcement point.

29
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.756
The application should know which layer
obtains the object,

30
00:01:37.836 --> 00:01:42.012
which layer evaluates richer rules, and
which layer performs the side effect.

31
00:01:42.092 --> 00:01:46.077
This prevents a route from checking one
object while a service later acts on

32
00:01:46.157 --> 00:01:46.663
another.

33
00:01:46.743 --> 00:01:50.349
A clear subject-action-object model
supports focused unit tests,

34
00:01:50.429 --> 00:01:52.516
integration tests across identities,

35
00:01:52.596 --> 00:01:57.341
and policy evolution without scattering
subtly different ownership checks throughout

36
00:01:57.421 --> 00:01:58.180
the codebase.
